# 5. MOTION DESIGN SYSTEM — CHERRYPICK, DON'T DECORATE

Do not simply install every animation library or copy every popular React component.

Select components based on what they communicate.

Study the libraries below and use the most appropriate elements.

### A. Motion for React

Reference: https://motion.dev/docs/react

Use for:
- Section entrance choreography.
- Carefully timed text and image reveals.
- Hover states.
- Spring-based interactions.
- Layout transitions.
- Shared visual transitions where appropriate.

Use this as the primary React-level motion system for normal interface animation.

### B. GSAP + ScrollTrigger

Reference: https://gsap.com/docs/v3/Plugins/ScrollTrigger/

Use for:
- The main classroom storytelling sequence.
- Pinned scroll-based architectural visualisations.
- Coordinated multi-element timelines.
- SVG energy paths.
- Scroll-controlled progression through the four-stage workflow.
- Transitions between architectural and software representations.

Use native document scrolling. Do not hijack the scrollbar or force users through an excessively long animation.

Limit pinned scenes to sections where they genuinely improve comprehension.

### C. React Three Fiber + Three.js + Drei

Reference: https://r3f.docs.pmnd.rs/getting-started/introduction

Use for:
- The signature classroom hero.
- The exploded-view architecture.
- A spatial transition from classroom to campus.
- Subtle camera movement and interactive architectural details.

Prefer a carefully crafted, lightweight scene over a visually noisy collection of 3D objects.

Inspect the existing React version and install compatible stable dependency versions. Do not upgrade the entire project blindly.

### D. Theatre.js — optional cinematic animation authoring

Reference: https://www.theatrejs.com/docs/latest

Evaluate Theatre.js only if it materially improves the timing and choreography of the 3D scene.

Use it for controlled scene timelines and precise camera/object animation where beneficial.

Do not introduce it just to increase the dependency count.

### E. Magic UI

References:

- https://magicui.design/docs/components/animated-beam
- https://magicui.design/docs/components/marquee
- https://magicui.design/docs/components

Cherrypick:

**Animated Beam:** Adapt the concept for the sensor → controller → appliance relationship. Use restrained energy pulses that travel along carefully drawn paths.

**Marquee:** Use for the BEYOND THE CLASSROOM logo section.

**Bento Grid:** May be adapted for one visual feature overview if it genuinely improves composition.

Do not use default gradients or styling blindly. Restyle components to match ROOMWISE.

### F. React Bits

References:

- https://reactbits.dev/c/backgrounds
- https://pro.reactbits.dev/docs/components

Study these effects as creative starting points:

- Topography — environmental and architectural contour patterns.
- Threads — fine flowing energy-like filaments.
- Silk / Pattern Waves — controlled surface movement.
- Gradient Waves — subtle ambient light transitions.
- Dotted Maps — campus-scale spatial patterns.

Adapt the effects to ROOMWISE's palette and meaning.

Do not indiscriminately use neon, glitch, CRT, galaxy, hyperspace, or random cursor effects.

Check whether a selected component requires a paid plan. Do not assume access to paid components or templates.

### G. Rive — optional state-driven animation

Reference: https://github.com/rive-app/rive-react

Consider Rive for a carefully authored interactive classroom diagram or a small, state-driven illustration.

It is especially suitable if a visual can change meaningfully in response to interaction.

Do not add Rive animations that simply decorate the screen.

### Final motion philosophy

One coherent motion language should govern the website.

Every animation must serve one of three purposes:

1. Explain how ROOMWISE works.
2. Establish the product's visual identity.
3. Guide attention through the story.

Use precise easing, natural acceleration, controlled delays and meaningful spatial movement.

The energy signal should be a recurring visual motif throughout the website.

Avoid:
- Random floating elements.
- Repetitive scroll reveals on every paragraph.
- Excessive parallax.
- Constantly moving backgrounds behind body text.
- Overly aggressive transitions.
- Cursor-following gimmicks.
- Unnecessary number-counting animations.
- Effects that interfere with reading or navigation.

