# 8. TECHNICAL QUALITY, PERFORMANCE AND ACCESSIBILITY

The website should look exceptional without becoming slow or fragile.

### Performance

- Lazy-load heavy 3D scenes.
- Use an efficient loading sequence for the hero.
- Use compressed textures and optimised 3D assets.
- Avoid unnecessarily high polygon counts.
- Avoid multiple WebGL canvases running simultaneously.
- Pause or reduce animation when scenes leave the viewport.
- Use appropriate fallbacks for devices that cannot render WebGL well.
- Avoid loading large video and 3D assets before they are required.
- Prevent layout shifts as scenes and media load.
- Preserve sensible mobile performance.

### Accessibility

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

Provide reduced-motion alternatives for major animated scenes and transitions.

Ensure:
- Keyboard-accessible navigation.
- Visible focus states.
- Sufficient text contrast.
- Semantic headings.
- Descriptive image alternatives.
- Accessible interactive controls.
- Captions for video where available.

Do not make essential information available only through motion or hover.

### Responsive design

Design deliberately for:
- Large desktop monitors.
- Standard laptop screens.
- Tablets.
- Mobile devices.

The desktop experience can be more immersive, but mobile must have its own carefully composed art direction rather than simply shrinking the desktop layout.

On mobile:
- Reduce or replace heavy 3D scenes where required.
- Preserve the narrative.
- Maintain readable typography.
- Simplify interactive controls.
- Keep navigation and CTAs accessible.

Test at 1920 × 1080, 1600 × 900, 1366 × 768, tablet dimensions and common mobile widths.

